---
const services = [
  { name: 'YouTube and Google', sends: 'Your session cookies and your searches', why: 'Feeds, search, videos, comments, notifications and account actions', optional: false },
  { name: 'GitHub', sends: 'No personal data', why: 'Downloads of yt-dlp and its JavaScript runtime, the daily update check, and the list of AI channels (only with the AI filter)', optional: false },
  { name: 'SponsorBlock', sends: 'Four characters of a hash of the video ID. The service cannot find which video you watch', why: 'Segments to skip', optional: true },
  { name: 'DeArrow titles', sends: 'Four characters of a hash of the video ID', why: 'Better titles', optional: true },
  { name: 'DeArrow thumbnails', sends: 'The video ID. This service can see which videos you look at', why: 'Better thumbnails', optional: true },
  { name: 'Return YouTube Dislike', sends: 'The video ID', why: 'Dislike counts', optional: true },
];
---

<section class="section" id="privacy">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="head">
      <p class="label" data-reveal>Privacy</p>
      <h2 class="title" data-reveal>Every service Velvet talks to</h2>
      <p class="lede" data-reveal>
        Velvet has no analytics and no telemetry, and it does not collect data about you. This is the full list of what
        the app connects to and what each service gets. You can turn off each optional one in the settings.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="table" role="table" aria-label="Services that Velvet connects to">
      <div class="tr th" role="row">
        <span role="columnheader">Service</span>
        <span role="columnheader">What Velvet sends</span>
        <span role="columnheader">Why</span>
      </div>
      {
        services.map((s) => (
          <div class="tr" role="row" data-row>
            <span role="cell" class="name">
              {s.name}
              {s.optional && <em>optional</em>}
            </span>
            <span role="cell">{s.sends}</span>
            <span role="cell">{s.why}</span>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <p class="note" data-reveal>
      Your sign-in stays on your Mac, in a file that only your macOS user account can read. Velvet sends it only to
      YouTube and Google. Velvet is open source, so you can read the code that does each of these things.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .table {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .tr {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 3fr) minmax(0, 5fr) minmax(0, 5fr);
    gap: 32px;
    padding-block: 16px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 22px;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .th {
    padding-block: 12px;
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 20px;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }

  .name {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .name em {
    display: block;
    font-style: normal;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 20px;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }

  .note {
    margin-top: 24px;
    max-width: 40rem;
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 22px;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .th {
      display: none;
    }

    .tr {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 4px;
    }

    .name em {
      display: inline;
      margin-left: 8px;
    }
  }
</style>
